From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: YanIanZ <iwayanbagusaryadarma@student.telkomuniversity.ac.id>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 00:15:51 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] SourbyCraft - relaxed region-ownership checks
 (LightingLuminol)

Owner decision 2026-10-22, verbatim after the risks were stated (data races, chunk corruption persisted to disk, item dupes, deadlocks): "Tiru penuh LightingLuminol". Adapted from LightingLuminol (GPL-3.0, Bacteriawa, revision 72d51c4d, minecraft patch 0001 "Base Dirty Patches"), where the throws are commented out unconditionally; here every check stays and asks guards/ThreadChecks (aurora.bridge.thread-checks: relaxed by default, bridged, enforced; effective only while aurora.bridge.mode admits plugins). RegionizedServer.ensureGlobalTickThread and the six RegionizedWorldData "not under this region's control" checks consult it; RegionizedServer.getCurrentTick and MinecraftServer.getTickCount answer with the global tick counter off every region (all modes); TickRegions.RegionStats tolerates a foreign thread; NearbyPlayers and EntityLookup (section-status guard, entity slices) lock only while checks are relaxed (static final, folded away otherwise); the RegionizedWorldData block-entity ticker hand-off, block events and the broadcast set lock their cross-thread touch points while the fields keep upstream's containers (LightingLuminol swapped the containers); redstoneTime is volatile; ServerChunkCache lets the global tick load a chunk synchronously while pumping global chunk tasks and keeps refusing a non-owning region thread (Folia#280); ChunkMap and the chunk tick loop tolerate holes a foreign removal leaves. A relaxed server is unsafe and unqualified; the boot log, /ver and /sourbycraft config say so.

